  <p>
    Stoicism is not a gloomy philosophy for people who never smile. It is the
    oldest trick for a hard moment, and it is one of the few ancient ideas that
    works on a five-year-old today. The whole thing fits in one line: you cannot
    always pick what happens, but you always pick what you do about it. Marcus
    Aurelius and Epictetus said that same line in different ways two thousand
    years ago, and a child can hold it and use it tonight.
  </p>

  <h2>The one idea a child can actually hold</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a child who drops the whole ice cream on the ground. They did not
    choose that. The heat and the pavement chose it. But now a real choice
    appears: rage, cry it out, turn it into a joke, or go find a grownup and get
    a new plan. None of that undoes the ice cream. But one of those choices
    makes the rest of the afternoon usable, and the others make it worse.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the whole Stoic idea, in a scene every parent knows. Draw a line
    down the middle of a page. On one side put what the child does not control:
    the weather, other people, most accidents, other kids' moods. On the other
    side put what they do control: their words, their effort, how they answer a
    hard moment. Stoicism says spend your energy on the second side and stop
    fighting the first. This is the fastest way to a calmer kid, and it works
    because it is true, not because it is clever.
  </p>

  <h2>Marcus Aurelius: the emperor who wrote notes to himself</h2>
  <p>
    Marcus Aurelius ran the Roman Empire, which means he had the opposite of a
    quiet life. People argued with him, wars happened, the weather ruined plans,
    and he could not fix most of it. So he wrote private reminders to himself.
    They were never meant to be published. They were a man talking himself down
    from a hard day, and they read like the things a father tells his son.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core reminder he returned to was this: a thing happens, and then the
    judgment about it is yours. He would tell himself that being angry about a
    thing changes the thing, not the anger, and it steals the day. The job is
    not to feel nothing. The job is to remember that the event is not the final
    word, your answer is. That is a sentence you can say to a child the moment
    a toy breaks: the toy is gone, and the rest of the afternoon is still yours
    to decide.
  </p>

  <h2>Epictetus: the teacher who started as a slave</h2>
  <p>
    Epictetus had one of the hardest starts a person can have, and he built a
    lifetime of teaching on one boundary. He drew the exact same line: some
    things are up to you, and some are not. His phrase for the first group is
    what is within our power, things like judgment, choice, and how we answer.
    His phrase for the second group is everything else, and he told people to
    stop treating that second group like it owes them anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    His most useful image for a child is the cup. A hard event is like a hand
    near the cup. It can push the cup, but it cannot make the cup overflow by
    itself. Only the person holding the cup decides how full it gets. That is
    why two children can lose the same game and one storms off while the other
    asks to play again. The game happened to both. The overflow is the part each
    one chose.
  </p>

