  </p>

  <h2>How to bring Stoicism to a young child</h2>
  <p>Three ways that never mention the word Stoicism and still teach the habit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Name the boundary out loud.</strong> The next time something small
      goes wrong, say the line in front of them. I did not choose this line at
      the store, but I choose how I stand in it. You cannot choose the line, but
      you choose how you stand in it. Saying it makes the invisible choice
      visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Praise the answer, not the event.</strong> When they lose a game
      and still ask to play again, say exactly what you saw. You lost and you
      still chose to come back. That is the strongest move there is. You are
      telling them the part worth being proud of is not the win, it is the
      choice they made when the win did not come.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask the one question.</strong> After a hard moment has cooled, ask
      a single question: what did you control there, and what did you not? The
      child who can answer that is halfway to the most useful habit an adult
      ever learns.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How this fits with the other thinkers in our books</h2>
  <p>
    The Curious Kid books work the same way. In one place the Curious Kid meets
    one thinker, and each thinker hands him one tool. Aristotle gives the right
    amount in the middle. Lao Tzu shows that opposites lean on each other.
    Stoicism would hand a child the tool for a hard day: what you cannot change
    is not the whole story, because you choose the answer.
  </p>
